# Workshop Visual & UX Standards

## Visual style

- Keep layouts **clean and modern** with **generous whitespace** — prefer breathing room over cramming content.
- Use the existing design tokens in `src/styles/global.css` (`--bg`, `--surface`, `--accent-clay`, `--accent-slate`, `--accent-sage`, `--text`, `--text-muted`, `--border`).
- Typography: **Fraunces** for display headings, **DM Sans** for body, **IBM Plex Mono** for labels/meta. Do not introduce new font families without explicit request.
- Reuse `--radius`, `--radius-organic`, and `--shadow` instead of one-off values.

```css
/* ✅ GOOD — token-based, consistent */
.card {
  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--border);
  padding: clamp(1rem, 3vw, 1.5rem);
}

/* ❌ BAD — random colors and tight spacing */
.card {
  background: #222;
  padding: 4px;
  font-family: Arial;
}
```

## Responsive design

- Support **desktop, tablet, and mobile**. Use fluid spacing (`clamp()`), flexible grids, and breakpoints already in `src/styles/components.css` (900px, 768px).
- Test touch targets: buttons and interactive elements ≥ 44px where possible.
- Avoid horizontal overflow; use `flex-wrap`, stacked layouts, and `max-width: var(--max-width)` for content.

## Preserve functionality

- When modifying visuals or copy, **do not break existing behavior** (nav scroll, Learn more expand/collapse, register form + toast, hero illustration interactions) unless the user explicitly asks to change it.
- Prefer CSS-only visual changes over refactoring component logic.

## Accessibility

- Maintain **sufficient color contrast** (WCAG AA): light text on dark surfaces, muted text still readable.
- Use semantic HTML (`header`, `main`, `section`, `nav`, `form`, `label`).
- Interactive controls need visible focus states, `aria-expanded` on toggles, and `aria-live` for dynamic feedback.
- Decorative SVGs: `aria-hidden="true"`. Meaningful controls: accessible names and keyboard support.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` — disable non-essential animations when set.

## Content & visuals

- Prefer visual elements (illustrations, icons, diagrams) over long text blocks when explaining concepts.
- Keep copy concise; let layout and visuals carry the story.
